To compare the two lists of tuples, convert them to dictionaries for key-value lookups. This reveals `t3` is missing in `external`, `t4` is newly added, and `t2`'s value changed from 200 to 250.

## Python Code
```python
internal = [("t1", 100), ("t2", 200), ("t3", 300)]
external = [("t1", 100), ("t2", 250), ("t4", 400)]

# Convert to dicts for comparison
internal_dict = dict(internal)
external_dict = dict(external)

# Validate missing in external
missing_in_external = set(internal_dict) - set(external_dict)
print("Missing in external:", missing_in_external)  # {'t3'}

# Find changes in common keys
changed = {}
common_keys = set(internal_dict) & set(external_dict)
for key in common_keys:
    if internal_dict[key] != external_dict[key]:
        changed[key] = (internal_dict[key], external_dict[key])

print("Changed:", changed)  # {'t2': (200, 250)}
